      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of both lines and use the property that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\).\n\n1. **Find the slope of the first line:**\n\nThe equation of the first line is already in slope-intercept form \\( y = 2x + 13 \\). \nBy comparing this to \\( y = mx + b \\), we identify the slope of the first line (denoted as \\( m_1 \\)):\n\\[\nm_1 = 2\n\\]\n\n2. **Find the slope of the second line:**\n\nThe second line is given by the equation:\n\\[\n6y + ax = 6\n\\]\n\nTo rewrite this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\n\nThis form indicates that the slope of this line (denoted as \\( m_2 \\)):\n\\[\nm_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\n\\]\n\n3. **Use the perpendicular condition:**\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\nSubstitute the slopes into this equation:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n\\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( a \\):**\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\tag 1\\number\n\\]\nSimplify by dividing both sides by \\(-2\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{3} = 1 \\tag 2 \\number\n\\]\nThen, multiply both sides by \\( 3 \\):\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).",
