      "text": "To determine if two lines are perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of the respective lines and check if their product is $-1$.\n\nFirst, notice that the equation $6y + ax = 6$ can be rewritten as $y = \\frac{-a}{6}x + 1$. The slope of this line is $\\frac{-a}{6}$, which is $-\\frac{a}{6}$.\n\nThe other line is given as $y = 2x + 13$, which has a slope of $2$.\n\nWe want these two lines to be perpendicular. Hence, the product of their slopes must be $-1$.\n\nConsider the equation $y = \\frac{a}{6}x + 2$, which will give us a line perpendicular to $y = \\frac{-a}{6}x + 1$.\n\nThe slope of this new line is $\\frac{a}{6}$, so we must have the product of the slopes equal to $-1$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n-\\frac{a}{6} \\cdot \\frac{a}{6} & = -1 \\\\\n-\\frac{a^2}{36} & = -1 \\\\\na^2 & = 36 \\\\\na & = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a = -6.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, the value of $a$ that makes the second line perpendicular to the first (and so the lines intersect at a $90^\\circ$ angle) is either $6$ or $-6$.",
